<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Montfort</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1779 CE (approx.) · capital Tettnang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lake Constance county of a branch of the counts of Tübingen, who founded Feldkirch and ruled Bregenz and Tettnang before selling out to Austria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hugo, a younger son of the Palatine Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, received lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Rhaetia</a> around 1200 and named himself after Montfort castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a>. He founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feldkirch">Feldkirch</a>. His descendants divided into several lines, including Werdenberg, Feldkirch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tettnang">Tettnang</a>, holding lands around the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled castles, small towns and Alpine valleys, often allied with or dependent on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. One of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-von-montfort">Hugo von Montfort</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a> line, was a noted poet of the late 14th century. Successive debts forced sales: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feldkirch">Feldkirch</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1375 and Bregenz in stages in the 15th century, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tettnang">Tettnang</a> line as the main survivor.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Deeply indebted after building their palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tettnang">Tettnang</a>, the counts sold the remaining county to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1779, and the line died out in 1787. Tettnang passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and then to Württemberg in 1810, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a> remained Austrian.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1200 CE – 1779 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:11.52%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1779 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tettnang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hugo I of Montfort, Hugo von Montfort</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Austria, Germany, Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Montfort, Montfort-Tettnang</dd></dl></aside></div>
